s has an even number of quote marks, so gcc reads them as complete strings and gives no warnings, even with `-Wall -Wextra`.\n- Both versions give exact results for large N. Python's integers have no size limit, and I turned off its default cap on how many digits it will print. The C version adds numbers in chunks of 9 digits, so it doesn't overflow at f(94) the way a 64-bit integer would.\n\n**Testing:** gcc 13.2.0 in the container compiled it cleanly. For N = 0, 1, 2, 10, 93, 94, 100, 1000 and 20000, the C output was byte-for-byte the same as Python's. The container has no `python3`, so I ran the Python side on the host, which is also Python 3.12.3. It has not been run under Python inside the container.\n\nThe program expects a non-negative N.", "ttft_ms": 5181, "type": "result", "duration_ms": 30767, "uuid": "0feab38e-615b-4f1c-8c9c-bbb6003d80a9", "ttft_stream_ms": 1536, "time_to_request_ms": 139, "first_content_frame_ms": 1537, "queued_turn_count": 0, "result_index": 0}